// **Stage three of the ray budget: importance becomes ray counts.**
//
// Everything before this measured; this decides. It reads a tile's importance,
// the average across the screen, and what the tile was given last frame, and
// writes the counts the tracing passes will use.
//
// **The rule is the design's, verbatim: a tile's share is its weight over the
// sum of all weights.** With a fixed tile count that sum is the mean times the
// count, so a tile's share of an average-rays-per-pixel budget is its weight
// divided by the mean. A tile of average interest gets exactly the average;
// twice as interesting gets twice as many, until the clamps say otherwise.
//
// **The budget is fixed, not reactive, and that is the whole of the stability
// story.** What came before was a frame-time controller stepping one global
// scale between four levels: every step changed the whole screen's quality at
// once, and on a scene near its budget it stepped continuously -- eight changes
// in one short session, measured. Nothing here reads frame time. The total is a
// setting; only its *distribution* moves, per tile, a ray at a time. A budget
// that cannot hunt cannot flicker.
//
// **Separate budgets per ray type, because they are not interchangeable.** One
// pooled number lets whichever effect is expensive today eat the others' rays,
// so turning on a feature quietly degrades two more. It is also what lets one
// allocator serve baked and realtime alike: with the bounce baked there is no
// runtime GI demand, and its budget is simply unspent rather than the whole
// dial idling at a level chosen for a load that is not there.
//
// **The dead band and the dwell (WR-16 S3, RAY-BUDGET-DESIGN 4.2).** What used
// to be here was an easing: a count could move one ray a frame toward its
// target. That is a damped integrator feeding a quantiser -- floor(x + 0.5) --
// which is the classic shape for a slow limit cycle, and this engine has
// already met one, breathing at about a hertz. Damping the input to a stair
// does not stop the stair being climbed; it makes the climbing rhythmic, which
// is why budget.Advance() was restored and reverted three times and made the
// picture worse every time.
//
// So the easing is gone and a hysteresis takes its place. A tile holds the
// whole number it has until the continuous target has moved a whole DeadBand
// away from it, and then takes the target's rounded value in one step; and it
// may not do that again for DwellFrames frames. Two properties fall out of the
// numbers rather than out of tuning:
//
//  * At the design's 0.75 the value after a step is at most 0.5 from the
//    target, which is inside the band -- so a step can never provoke the step
//    back. Noise under three quarters of a ray moves nothing at all.
//  * The step is to the *rounded target*, not one ray toward it. Limiting the
//    size of a step only adds latency, and latency is exactly what the easing
//    was for; the band already does the job the easing was trying to do.

#type vertex
#version 450 core

layout(location = 0) out vec2 v_UV;

void main()
{
	v_UV = vec2((gl_VertexIndex << 1) & 2, gl_VertexIndex & 2);
	gl_Position = vec4(v_UV * 2.0 - 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
}

#type fragment
#version 450 core

layout(location = 0) in vec2 v_UV;
layout(location = 0) out vec4 o_Budget;

// This frame's raw tile map: importance in r, luminance in g.
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D u_Tiles;
// Its 1x1 reduction. Only `.r` matters -- the mean importance.
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D u_Mean;
// Last frame's output of this pass: the counts to hold, and how long each has
// been held. Point-sampled by the caller -- a filtered read of a packed
// counter is a number that means nothing.
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) uniform sampler2D u_History;

layout(push_constant) uniform Params
{
	float TexelX;
	float TexelY;
	float AoAverage;       // A: rays per pixel the AO budget can afford
	float GiAverage;       // B: and the same for the bounce
	float HasHistory;      // C
	float FlipY;

	float MinFactor;       // the least of its share a tile may be cut to
	float MaxFactor;       // and the most it may be given
	float DeadBand;        // how far the target must move before a count does
	float AoCeiling;
	float GiCeiling;
	float DwellFrames;     // and how long a count must then sit still
	float Smoothing;       // how much of this frame's demand to believe
} u_Budget;

// Both dwell counters share the alpha lane, so each is capped where the pair
// still lands on an exactly-representable half: 31 + 32 * 31 = 1023, and a
// half float is exact to 2048. The dwell that matters is 8.
const float kDwellCap = 31.0;
const float kDwellPack = 32.0;
// A tile counts as sky when its averaged demand falls this far under the
// screen's mean. An average approaches zero without reaching it, so the
// test needs a floor; at the default smoothing a tile that genuinely
// becomes sky crosses this in about fifty frames, and costs nothing in
// the meantime -- both tracing passes return on an empty pixel before
// they read this map (rtao_compute:253, rtgi_trace:218).
const float kEmptyFraction = 1.0e-3;

// One lane's decision: the count it keeps, and how many frames it has kept it.
//
// Held while the target is inside the band or the dwell has not run out;
// otherwise the target's rounded value, and the dwell restarts.
vec2 Hold(float held, float target, float dwell, float band, float wanted)
{
	// **Capped here, because here is where the cap is known.** The counter
	// saturates at kDwellCap, so a dwell asked for above it is a dwell that
	// can never elapse: the test below stays true and the tile freezes for
	// ever. `--tile-dwell` clamps in the parser, but RenderSettings does not
	// and reaches this shader unchecked -- one of two entry points guarded,
	// which is this codebase's own recurring defect. Guarding the rule where
	// it is defined covers both, and whatever the third turns out to be.
	const float dwellFrames = min(wanted, kDwellCap);

	// **A tile that had nothing last frame takes its target at once.** Sky, or
	// ground a moving camera has just uncovered: the dwell exists to stop a
	// count walking, and there is no count to walk from. Without this a newly
	// covered tile traces nothing for the length of the dwell, which reads as
	// a band of unshaded tiles following the camera.
	if (held <= 0.0)
		return vec2(floor(target + 0.5), 0.0);

	if (abs(target - held) < band || dwell < dwellFrames)
		return vec2(held, min(dwell + 1.0, kDwellCap));

	const float next = floor(target + 0.5);
	// A band under half a ray can be crossed without the rounded value
	// changing. That is not a change and must not restart the dwell, or a tile
	// sitting near a boundary is held back for ever by its own near misses.
	return next == held ? vec2(held, min(dwell + 1.0, kDwellCap))
						: vec2(next, 0.0);
}

void main()
{
	const bool flip = u_Budget.FlipY > 0.5;
	const vec2 uv = vec2(v_UV.x, flip ? 1.0 - v_UV.y : v_UV.y);
	const vec2 texel = vec2(u_Budget.TexelX, u_Budget.TexelY);

	// **Smoothed across neighbours before anything is decided.** Importance is
	// measured per tile and tiles are independent, so a lone tile can read high
	// while its neighbours read low -- and a ray count that jumps at a tile
	// boundary draws that boundary into the picture, a 16-pixel grid of
	// differently-noisy squares. Blurring the *weights* costs nine fetches of a
	// tiny texture and makes the allocation continuous across the screen.
	float importance = 0.0;
	float weight = 0.0;
	for (int y = -1; y <= 1; y++)
	{
		for (int x = -1; x <= 1; x++)
		{
			const vec2 at = uv + vec2(x, y) * texel;
			if (any(lessThan(at, vec2(0.0))) || any(greaterThan(at, vec2(1.0))))
				continue;
			// The centre counts double: this is the tile being allocated for,
			// and its neighbours are context rather than equals.
			const float w = (x == 0 && y == 0) ? 2.0 : 1.0;
			importance += texture(u_Tiles, at).r * w;
			weight += w;
		}
	}
	importance /= max(weight, 1.0);

	const float mean = max(texelFetch(u_Mean, ivec2(0), 0).r, 1.0e-5);

	// **Last frame's decisions, read once**: the two counts, how long each has
	// been held, and the demand this tile has been averaging.
	const bool hasHistory = u_Budget.HasHistory > 0.5;
	const vec4 was = hasHistory ? texture(u_History, uv) : vec4(0.0);

	// **The demand is averaged across frames, and that is what made the
	// allocator still.** Measured: with TAA's jitter off, this allocator does
	// not change a single tile in sixty seconds, on the night bridge or the
	// showroom. With it on, nothing but a dead band wide enough to freeze the
	// allocator reached the bar -- and the width needed differed per scene (4
	// rays on the bridge, 8 in the showroom), because the wobble scales with
	// what a tile is asking for. A threshold in rays was never going to fit
	// both.
	//
	// The wobble has a cause and a period: the camera is nudged a fraction of
	// a pixel per frame over a fixed cycle (RenderSettings::TemporalJitterPhase),
	// which moves silhouettes across the importance pass's sample lattice.
	// Averaging over frames cancels what repeats and keeps what does not, so
	// what reaches the clamps below is the tile's real demand and the design's
	// own 0.75 band is wide enough for it again.
	//
	// One-pole rather than a true box over the cycle: a box cancels a periodic
	// signal exactly but wants a frame of storage per step, and this is one
	// lane. Smoothing is the weight of *this* frame -- 1 believes it whole,
	// which is the undamped allocator this replaces.
	const float demand = hasHistory
					   ? mix(was.b, importance, clamp(u_Budget.Smoothing, 0.0, 1.0))
					   : importance;

	// Clamped at both ends. The floor matters as much as the ceiling: a tile
	// cut to nothing produces no new information, so it cannot become
	// interesting again and stays cut. The ceiling stops one glinting tile
	// spending a budget the rest of the screen paid for.
	const float share = clamp(demand / mean,
							  u_Budget.MinFactor, u_Budget.MaxFactor);

	// **The target stays continuous and is clamped before it is made whole.**
	// Rounding first and clamping after would leave the band measuring a
	// distance to a number the clamps forbid: a tile pinned at the ceiling
	// would read as permanently far from its target and step every time the
	// dwell allowed it.
	//
	// **A tile with no surface targets zero, and the averaged demand decides
	// whether it has one.** Testing this frame's own reading was the largest
	// single source of restlessness before the averaging: a tile holding a
	// bridge cable lands no sample on geometry in some frames, and a zero is a
	// whole ray count away from what its neighbours get -- the biggest step
	// this allocator can take, and one no dead band can damp because zero was
	// reached by a different path. An average never collapses to zero for a
	// tile that is covered five frames in eight, so the crossing stops
	// happening rather than being rationed.
	const bool empty = demand <= mean * kEmptyFraction;
	const float aoTarget = empty ? 0.0
						 : clamp(u_Budget.AoAverage * share, 1.0, u_Budget.AoCeiling);
	const float giTarget = empty ? 0.0
						 : clamp(u_Budget.GiAverage * share, 1.0, u_Budget.GiCeiling);

	// Rounded rather than truncated: truncation biases every tile down and the
	// budget quietly under-spends by half a ray a tile. This is also the whole
	// of the first frame, which has nothing to hold.
	vec2 ao = vec2(floor(aoTarget + 0.5), 0.0);
	vec2 gi = vec2(floor(giTarget + 0.5), 0.0);

	if (hasHistory)
	{
		const float packed = max(was.a, 0.0);
		const float giDwell = floor(packed / kDwellPack);
		const float aoDwell = packed - giDwell * kDwellPack;

		ao = Hold(was.r, aoTarget, aoDwell, u_Budget.DeadBand, u_Budget.DwellFrames);
		gi = Hold(was.g, giTarget, giDwell, u_Budget.DeadBand, u_Budget.DwellFrames);
	}

	// `.b` carried the smoothed importance for a debug view that was never
	// written, then briefly a coverage age; it carries the averaged demand,
	// which is what made the age unnecessary. `.a` carried the share, which
	// nothing read, and carries the two dwell counters. Both diagnostics
	// became state the moment the allocator needed a memory, and neither had
	// a reader to lose.
	o_Budget = vec4(ao.x, gi.x, demand,
					clamp(ao.y, 0.0, kDwellCap) + kDwellPack * clamp(gi.y, 0.0, kDwellCap));
}
